•fauthorxApub:sha2-256:tgaiwo4ow4sfqfopfr7vk3oqtnz5au2z74qzje7xbvvwgzwvx7fadbodyßeclassharchivalgcontentxppubrel 0.5.0 was released on 2026-10-09 from commit b6b4582d2c851dfe55ef5506ef829257575fc4c6 with these changes.ddata¢gcolumnsÇ°dnamehcategory°dnamefchangedrowsçÇhinternalyRDocumentation brought towards pubkit's Rust crate standard by its rust-crate-quality agent: exact `# Errors` sections and docs for every item other modules use, `// covers:` comments on the unit tests, a README covering commands, why pubrel, related tools, versioning and its versioned interface, MSRV and security, and a new SECURITY.md.Çgchangedxipubrel is licensed under Apache-2.0, as publet is, instead of MIT, and the licence text ships as LICENSE.Çefixedx˚`pubrel --help` (and `-h`, `help`) prints the usage to stdout and succeeds; it failed as an unknown command, with the usage on stderr. The usage now lists `summary` among the commands --package applies to, and the --package=NAME and version spellings.Çhinternalx”pubrel depends on publet-core 0.1.2 from crates.io instead of a git tag, and its manifest carries crates.io metadata and an include list, so it can be published there. Scenarios cover init, --version and --help.ÇefixedxÀA version whose part to bump is already u64::MAX is refused with a message, instead of panicking: pubrel's release builds check for overflow, so a pull request could make `check` crash. Found by fuzzing.Çhinternalx´What `check` reads from a pull request is fuzzed: release.json, unreleased.json, the corpus lock and the manifests with the version arithmetic. CI runs it on every change.Çhinternalyupubrel's dependencies are checked in CI and before each release: cargo deny (advisories, licences, sources) and cargo vet (audits imported from Mozilla, Google, the Bytecode Alliance, Zcash, ISRG and Embark Studios, trusted publishers, or exemptions with their evidence). SUPPLY-CHAIN.md ships in the package, and each release records the checks in a signed security claim.Çefixedx≤pubrel declared Rust 1.98, the toolchain it is developed with, though it builds on 1.88, the first release with stable `let` chains. It now declares 1.88, and CI builds it there.Çhinternalx∏CI builds pubrel on its minimum Rust version, runs the tests on 32-bit (i686) and big-endian (s390x) targets, and runs nightly, with longer fuzzing and the latest stable and beta Rust.Çefixedy<A security check's recorded summary is the last line it printed to stdout. stdout and stderr were joined with stderr last, so a tool that warns on stderr after its summary, as cargo deny does for duplicate crates, had a warning recorded in place of its result. Facts and tool versions are read from stdout first too.Çeaddedyp`pubrel withdraw PR|BRANCH` withdraws a release that was cut but never merged: it lists the objects that release added in .draft-discards, which pub never exports, so the next release does not carry them. `prepare` and `check` refuse a release carrying records of another attempt, and `prepare` says what to undo when a release cut again reproduces a withdrawn object.Çhinternalxì`make publish-check` verifies the package in its own target directory: sharing target/ left cargo reporting pubrel up to date after source changes.Çeaddedx‘`pubrel withdraw PR` refuses an open or merged pull request, and deletes a closed one's release branch from origin and from this clone (unless it is a fork's), so the release can be cut again under the same name.gdependsÅxApub:sha2-256:3dqa5xb4w646r6u6oihwq3mu3xnfiq4ghpn542phgjgxhbhfwdvahevidenceÅ§dkindhexternalcreffpubrelhrevisionx(b6b4582d2c851dfe55ef5506ef829257575fc4c6drolefsourcedlangbenescope¢jconditionsÄfdomainx&the published release v0.5.0 of pubrelgcreatedt2026-10-09T00:00:00Zcpuba1dtypekclaim.prose